          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Three urine infections in one year is worth raising with a clinician. The red-flag check did not match an emergency pattern for your description. That does not mean nothing is wrong, so if you feel very unwell, get confused or drowsy, have fever with shivering, or pass very little urine, seek urgent care straight away. Those signs can mean a serious infection spreading (sepsis), according to the sepsis guidance in the emergency-medicine reference.\n\nThe guidance I can see does not directly cover what counts as \"recurrent\" or when to escalate. It does say that a urine culture (a lab test to identify the bacteria) is used in certain cases, including recurrent infection, symptoms that come back within a few weeks of treatment, and recent antibiotic use. This comes from the infectious diseases and urology guidance, \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence.\"\n\nThe general reference, \"Urinary tract infection,\" lists factors that contribute to infections, including incomplete bladder emptying, diabetes, kidney stones, certain contraceptives including spermicides and diaphragms, and previous infections. It also says that managing infections depends on local resistance patterns, meaning how well local bacteria respond to particular antibiotics.\n\nThe passages I was given do not cover prevention strategies for repeated infections, and they do not say how many infections in a year counts as needing specialist referral. Those are good questions for your GP or pharmacist. You could ask whether a urine test is needed to confirm the cause, and which factors on the list might apply to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
